```java
// Single lock: every increment serializes through one point of contention
class LockedCounter {
    private long count = 0;
    private final Object lock = new Object();
    void increment() {
        synchronized (lock) { count++; }
    }
    long get() { synchronized (lock) { return count; } }
}

// Sharded: threads mostly hit their own shard, contention only on read
class ShardedCounter {
    private final long[] shards = new long[16];  // one per likely-contending thread group
    void increment(int threadHash) {
        int i = threadHash % shards.length;
        synchronized (shards) { shards[i]++; }   // still needs sync per-shard in real code
    }
    long get() {
        long total = 0;
        for (long s : shards) total += s;         // summed only when a total is needed
        return total;
    }
}
```

Under high concurrency, `LockedCounter` funnels every single increment through one lock — exactly the serial fraction Amdahl's Law penalizes. `ShardedCounter` spreads increments across 16 independent shards, so two threads hashing to different shards never contend at all; only `get()`, called far less often, pays a cost proportional to shard count. The trade: `get()` is slower and the true count can be momentarily stale across shards mid-update — acceptable for most counters (page views, request counts), not for anything requiring an exact, immediately-consistent total.
